7592186050603 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050603 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050603 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050603 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050603 #edb/ref 1031 "Rotation-Scaling Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050603 #edb/ref 1033 2017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050603 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "579b98fc-d572-5a6a-9016-eb840c350a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1031 "Combining Linear Transformations Using 2x2 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1033 933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1034 1.3435310991001899 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050646 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5c07d79b-5ef8-558c-8045-01d5e9a852a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087567 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087567 #edb/ref 1071 "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](images/03/0394b5d3a371caf95d5e701c0ffb00894eb685e706d8b70af2149057de0b0cb5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8559e2bf-8b48-5e26-81c6-0db03d93645a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087567 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186087568 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087568 #edb/ref 1071 "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](images/bd/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](images/be/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef17b637-7c4d-5515-99b7-13b1290244f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087568 #edb/ref 1074 "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](images/48/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](images/96/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186087751 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087751 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921862