7592186560997 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560997 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560997 #edb/ref 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560997 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f9de987-bdcf-56a6-b2f7-abab5cba462a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560997 #edb/ref 1074 "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{5}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561057 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561057 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561057 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561057 #edb/ref 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561057 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9e04c9b-0512-5399-9fe0-561c4a297b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561057 #edb/ref 1074 "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{2}{3}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
